```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies entirely on router16, not on mswitch0 as the ticket claims. On
router16, the FRR OSPF daemon (ospfd) is not running at all — confirmed by
"ospfd is not running" from vtysh and an FRR running-config that contains only
bare boilerplate (no interface statements, no router ospf block, nothing).
Router16's IP addresses (bond0 = 10.2.2.1/24 toward mswitch0, eth2 =
172.30.1.1/24 toward router7) are configured at the kernel level via the
startup script, but without ospfd those interfaces are never injected into the
OSPF domain.

Because router16's ospfd is absent, it cannot send or receive OSPF Hello
packets on either of its transit links. As a result, mswitch0 has zero OSPF
neighbors on every interface (br0, vlan10, vlan20, vlan30 all show Neighbor
Count 0), so it has no path to distribute its Campus 1 VLAN prefixes
(172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, 172.16.30.0/24) into the OSPF domain.
Symmetrically, router7 (the backbone aggregation router) has a full OSPF table
of all other subnets but contains no entry for any 172.16.x.x prefix — meaning
return traffic from Campus 2 or the backbone toward Campus 1 is dropped at
router7.

Campus 1 PCs can still reach their local VLAN gateways on mswitch0 because
those are directly connected — but any packet destined beyond mswitch0 is
blackholed due to the missing reverse path.

Root cause: The ospfd process was not started (and no OSPF configuration
exists) on router16, severing the OSPF adjacency chain mswitch0 ↔ router16 ↔
router7 that bridges Campus 1 to the rest of the network.

Fix: Restore a valid FRR OSPF configuration on router16 — add "router ospf",
a router-id, and "network" statements covering 10.2.2.0/24 and 172.30.1.0/24
in area 0, then start ospfd. Once adjacencies form with mswitch0 and router7,
the Campus 1 VLAN routes will propagate through the backbone and reachability
will be fully restored.
=== END ===
```